This project has a graphify knowledge graph at .graphify/ (canonical file `.graphify/graph.json`; the legacy `graphify-out` directory is not used). Contract: `docs/AYAS_GRAPHIFY_INTEGRATION.md`.

Before using Read, Grep, Glob, or Bash to explore the codebase, orient with graphify first — and include this rule in every subagent prompt that explores code:
- `graphify query "<question>"` — scoped subgraph for any codebase or architecture question
- `graphify path "<A>" "<B>"` — dependency path between two symbols
- `graphify explain "<concept>"` — all nodes related to a concept, including what depends on it
- `graphify review-analysis --files <changed files> --graph .graphify/graph.json` — blast radius of a change

Use Read/Grep/Glob directly when graphify has already oriented you and you need specific lines, when `.graphify/graph.json` does not exist, or for the `.ps1` startup scripts (Graphify has no PowerShell grammar installed here, so the graph has no nodes for them).

- Check freshness with `npx tsx scripts/ayas-graphify-status.ts`; if `STRUCTURAL_STATUS` is STALE, refresh before relying on the graph. A pending semantic description marker alone does not block.
- If `.graphify/wiki/index.md` exists, navigate it instead of reading raw files
- Read `.graphify/GRAPH_REPORT.md` only for broad architecture review when query/path/explain do not surface enough context
- After modifying code files, run `graphify update --scope all --no-description --no-label .` to keep the graph current (AST-only, local, no API cost)
